Text:
Drugs used to treat type 2 diabetes include metformin, which activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) to enhance glucose uptake and reduce hepatic glucose production. Sulfonylureas like glipizide stimulate insulin secretion by closing ATP-sensitive potassium channels in pancreatic beta cells. DPP-4 inhibitors (e.g., sitagliptin) increase GLP-1 levels by inhibiting the enzyme dipeptidyl peptidase-4, thereby enhancing insulin release and reducing glucagon. SGLT2 inhibitors (e.g., empagliflozin) block sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 in the kidneys, promoting glucose excretion in urine. GLP-1 receptor agonists (e.g., liraglutide) mimic GLP-1, improving insulin secretion and reducing appetite. Thiazolidinediones (e.g., pioglitazone) activate peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-γ), enhancing insulin sensitivity in adipose and muscle tissues. Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors (e.g., acarbose) delay carbohydrate digestion, reducing postprandial glucose spikes. Meglitinides (e.g., repaglinide) also target ATP-sensitive potassium channels but act more rapidly than sulfonylureas. These drugs collectively address insulin resistance, secretion, and glucose reabsorption to manage hyperglycemia.

